---
import MainLayout from '../../layouts/MainLayout.astro';
---

<MainLayout
  title="Internal apps should be private by default - OmniR3"
  description="Cloudflare Access can now protect a Worker on every hostname it uses, including previews. Here is why that matters for internal software."
  currentPath="/insights"
>
  <article>
    <section class="section page-head">
      <div class="container article-head">
        <p class="eyebrow">Insights · August 18, 2026</p>
        <h1>Internal apps should be private by default</h1>
        <p class="lead">
          Cloudflare can now attach Access to a Worker itself — not just to each
          hostname — so internal software stays behind company login even when
          someone adds a new domain or ships a preview.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section section-alt">
      <div class="container prose">
        <p>
          AI-assisted development has made it easy for almost anyone to stand up
          an internal tool and put it on the public internet. That speed is
          useful. It is also the gap that keeps security teams awake: a
          prototype that was never meant to be public, sitting on a preview URL
          or a forgotten custom domain, with company data behind it.
        </p>
        <p>
          Cloudflare’s answer, announced in
          <a
            href="https://blog.cloudflare.com/workers-protected-by-access/"
            rel="noopener noreferrer"
            target="_blank"
          >
            Access for Workers</a
          >, is to bind the access policy to the application rather than to the
          DNS name. Authentication runs before Worker code. If Access is on, the
          request does not reach the app until the user has signed in — whether
          they arrived through a custom domain, a route, a workers.dev
          subdomain, or a preview URL.
        </p>

        <h2>Why hostname policies were not enough</h2>
        <p>
          Previously, Access had to be configured per hostname. Add a new custom
          domain to a Worker and you also had to update the Access application,
          or that hostname was reachable without a login. That is a classic
          secure-by-hope failure: the default is open unless someone remembers
          the extra step.
        </p>
        <p>
          Attaching the policy to the Worker closes that gap. You can protect
          preview URLs only, which is the right default when production is
          intentionally public, or protect every hostname associated with the
          Worker. Account-level policy can apply the same rule to every current
          and future Worker, with an explicit bypass when a service needs to be
          public.
        </p>

        <h2>Identity without a homemade JWT check</h2>
        <p>
          When Access is protecting the Worker, identity is available on the
          request context. Teams can read email, name, and groups with
          <code>ctx.access.getIdentity()</code> instead of parsing and verifying a
          JWT themselves. That is less glue code, and fewer places to get signature
          validation wrong. Local development can simulate an authenticated user in
          Wrangler so permission checks can be tested before a deploy.
        </p>
        <p>
          For internal platforms, the same idea scales. Put Access on a Workers
          for Platforms dispatch Worker and every app deployed through that
          namespace inherits the gate. That is the model we want for
          employee-built tools: private by default, not private if someone
          remembered to click the right checkbox.
        </p>

        <h2>What we recommend</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>
            Make preview deployments private even when production is public.
            Drafts should never be the leak.
          </li>
          <li>
            Prefer account-wide or platform-wide defaults over per-app setup
            when many people can deploy.
          </li>
          <li>
            Treat public access as an explicit exception, not the starting
            state.
          </li>
          <li>
            Use the identity already established at the edge to personalize,
            authorize, and audit — do not rebuild that trust inside the app.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          This is the same standard we apply in delivery: reliable systems are
          not only well tested, they fail closed. If your organization is
          shipping internal tools on Workers — or standing up a platform so
          other teams can — Access on the Worker is now the straightforward way
          to keep those apps behind company identity.
        </p>

        <p class="source-note">
          Source:{' '}
          <a
            href="https://blog.cloudflare.com/workers-protected-by-access/"
            rel="noopener noreferrer"
            target="_blank"
          >
            Cloudflare — Secure all your internal vibe-coded applications, in
            one click
          </a>
        </p>
        <p>
          <a class="text-link" href="/insights">Back to Insights</a>
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="cta-band">
      <div class="container">
        <h2>Need internal software that is private by default?</h2>
        <a class="btn btn-primary" href="/contact">Talk with OmniR3</a>
      </div>
    </section>
  </article>
</MainLayout>
